From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 08:20:29 +0100
Subject: [PATCH] webOS: send voice notes, mentions and contact cards as
 Signal's own

* Send recorded voice messages as audio voice notes

The Messaging app records a voice message with
com.webos.service.mediarecorder, which writes an M4A (AAC in an MP4
container) into /media/internal/.im-attachments/. The attachment's
content type came from sniffing the bytes, which calls any MP4
container video/mp4, so the recipient saw a video.

An .m4a now goes out as audio/mp4, and one recorded straight in that
folder also carries the voice-note flag, so it shows as a voice
message. imlibpurpleservice leaves these M4A files as they are for
Signal (it converts them to Ogg/Opus for the other services).

* Send @-mentions from the app as Signal mentions

A message the user writes in the Messaging app can mention people. The
transport sets the conversation data "webos-send-mentions" around
serv_send_im and serv_chat_send: records "<id>\x1f<name>" joined by
"\x1e", where id is the contact's buddy name here (the ACI, or a phone
number) and the text holds "@<name>" for each. The transport frees it
right after the send returns, so presage_rust_send copies it before it
returns; a file send passes none.

Signal sends a mention as U+FFFC in the body with a BodyRange over it,
start and length in UTF-16 code units, naming the ACI. mentions.rs does
the replacement: each mention takes one "@<name>" of its own, longer
names first, so that "@Anna" stays Anna's when Ann is mentioned as
well. A phone-number id is resolved to the contact as a recipient is;
an id that is no contact, or a name not found in the text, is left as
the "@<name>" text it was. The mentions are placed before the "@needle:"
reply form is looked for, so a text starting with a mention is not
mistaken for that form.

What the app shows for the sent message, and the text handed back with
"webos-im-outbox-id", stay the plain text. mentions.rs has its own
tests (rustc --test mentions.rs).

* Send a shared contact card as a Signal contact

The Messaging app can share a contact card. The transport sets the
card, a vCard 3.0 with FN and TEL lines, as the conversation data
"webos-send-contact" around serv_send_im and serv_chat_send and sends
its plain-text form (the name and the numbers) as the message text.
send_im and chat_send hand it to presage_rust_send, which copies it
before it returns, as it does the mentions.

Signal has its own form for this, the DataMessage contact field, which
its clients show as a contact card. vcard.rs reads the name and the
numbers from the card: lines unfolded, escapes undone, the first card
only, TEL kinds from TYPE=cell/home/work or the bare vCard 2.1 forms,
another type kept as a custom label and a number without one taken for
a mobile number. The FN goes into the contact's givenName, as the card
has no parts for it. Such a message goes as the contact alone, as
Signal clients send one, without the text, which would only repeat it.
A card that cannot be read is sent as that text.

The location the app can attach ("webos-send-location") has no Signal
form of its own, so it still goes as the text the transport made of
it. vcard.rs has its own tests (rustc --test vcard.rs).

Signed-off-by: Herman van Hazendonk <github.com@herrie.org>
Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [webOS specific]
